      "text": "Since the sequence $10, a, b, ab$ is an arithmetic progression, the difference between consecutive terms must be constant. Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\\[b - a = a - b = ab - b.\\]\nFrom the first two parts of the equation, we have:\n\\[2b - 2a = 2a \\Rightarrow b = a + a = 2a.\\]\nFrom the last part, we have:\n\\[ab - b = b \\Rightarrow ab = 2b \\Rightarrow a = 2.\\]\nSubstituting $a = 2$ into $b = 2a$, we get:\n\\[b = 2(2) = 4.\\]\nThus, the only pair $(a, b)$ that satisfies the arithmetic progression property is $(2, 4)$. Therefore, there is only $1$ pair $(a, b)$ that works.\nThe answer is: 1",
