      "text": "To determine the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression, we need to use the property of arithmetic progressions. In an arithmetic progression, the difference between consecutive terms is constant. Let's denote this common difference by \\(d\\).\n\nGiven the terms \\(10, a, b, ab\\), we can write the following equations based on the property of arithmetic progressions:\n\n1. \\(a = 10 + d\\)\n2. \\(b = a + d = 10 + 2d\\)\n3. \\(ab = b + d = b + d\\)\n\nFrom the third equation, we have:\n\\[ ab = b + d \\]\n\\[ ab = b + d \\]\n\\[ ab - b = d \\]\n\\[ b(a - 1) = d \\]\n\nFrom the first equation, we have:\n\\[ a = 10 + d \\]\n\nNow substitute \\(a = 10 + d\\) into the equation \\(b(a - 1) = d\\):\n\\[ b(10 + d - 1) = d \\]\n\\[ b(9 + d) = d \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{d}{9 + d} \\]\n\nSince \\(d\\) is a common difference in the arithmetic progression, it must be a real number. Therefore, we need to ensure that \\(b\\) is a real number. This means the denominator \\(9 + d\\) must not be zero:\n\\[ 9 + d \\neq 0 \\]\n\\[ d \\neq -9 \\]\n\nThus, \\(d\\) can be any real number except \\(-9\\). For each valid \\(d\\), we can find \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) as follows:\n\\[ a = 10 + d \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{d}{9 + d} \\]\n\nThe number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) is infinite because \\(d\\) can be any real number except \\(-9\\). However, if we are asked for the number of distinct pairs \\((a, b)\\) without considering the range of \\(d\\), we recognize that there are infinitely many such pairs.\n\nTherefore, the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression is \\(\\boxed{\\infty}\\).",
