      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "An arithmetic progression (AP) is a sequence of numbers in which the difference of any two successive members is a constant. Given that $10, a, b, ab$ is an arithmetic progression, the common difference $d$ between consecutive terms must be the same.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be expressed in two different ways based on the sequence:\n1. $a - 10 = d$\n2. $b - a = d$\n3. $ab - b = d$\n4. $ab - a = d$\n\nFrom the first condition, $a - 10 = d$. Substituting $d$ into the second condition, we get $b - a = a - 10$. This simplifies to $b - a = a - 10$ or $b = 2a - 10$.\n\nSubstituting $a = 2a - 10$ into the sequence, we get:\n\\[ a = 2(2a - 10) - 10 \\]\n\\[ a = 4a - 20 - 10 \\]\n\\[ a = 4a - 30 \\]\n\\[ 3a = 30 \\]\n\\[ a = 10 \\]\n\nNow, substituting $a = 10$ back into the equation $b = 2a - 10$, we get:\n\\[ b = 2(10) - 10 \\]\n\\[ b = 20 - 10 \\]\n\\[ b = 10 \\]\n\nNext, we need to check if $ab$ can be a term in the arithmetic progression. Since $a = 10$ and $b = 10$, we find $ab = 10 \\cdot 10 = 100$. We need to check if $100$ can be the third term in the sequence $10, a, b, ab$.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can also be expressed as:\n\\[ d = \\frac{b - a}{2} = \\frac{10 - 10}{2} = 0 \\]\n\\[ d = \\frac{ab - a}{2} = \\frac{100 - 10}{2} = \\frac{90}{2} = 45 \\]\n\\[ d = \\frac{ab - b}{2} = \\frac{100 - 10}{2} = \\frac{90}{2} = 45 \\]\n\nSince the common difference $d$ is consistent, $100$ can indeed be the third term in the sequence. Therefore, $(a, b) = (10, 10)$ is a valid pair.\n\nWe need to check if there are any other possible pairs. The common difference $d$ must be consistent with the arithmetic progression, and we have already found that $d = 0$ for the equality to hold. This means that $10, a, b, ab$ must be an arithmetic progression with a common difference of $0$, and we have already used this condition to derive $a = 10$ and $b = 10$. Any other values of $a$ and $b$ would not satisfy the condition of forming an arithmetic progression with a constant difference.\n\nThus, there is only one pair $(a, b)$ that satisfies the conditions of the problem, and that pair is $(10, 10)$. Therefore, the number of such pairs is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).",
